{
  "title": "Boss: The full report",
  "summary": "Add a histogram built from Range and new string, and list missing coursework with Except.",
  "minutes": 20,
  "kind": "boss",
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Boss briefing: the full report",
      "body": [
        "Two last sections turn the gradebook into a report a teacher would actually read.",
        "",
        "**Score bands** are a text histogram. `Enumerable.Range(6, 4)` makes the numbers 6, 7, 8, 9; `Reverse()` puts 90s first; `new string('#', n)` draws a bar of `n` characters:",
        "",
        "```csharp fragment",
        "var bands = Enumerable.Range(6, 4)",
        "    .Reverse()",
        "    .Select(d => (Label: $\"{d * 10}s\", Count: scores.Count(s => Math.Min(s.Points / 10, 9) == d)))",
        "    .Append((\"<60\", scores.Count(s => s.Points < 60)));",
        "```",
        "",
        "`Math.Min(..., 9)` folds a perfect 100 into the 90s band.",
        "",
        "**Missing coursework** is a set difference. `Except` returns the items of the first sequence that are not in the second:",
        "",
        "```csharp fragment",
        "var missing = allCourses.Except(studentCourses);",
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "Drawing bars from numbers. What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "var counts = new[] { 3, 0, 1 };",
        "",
        "foreach (var i in Enumerable.Range(0, counts.Length))",
        "    Console.WriteLine($\"{i}|{new string('#', counts[i])}\");"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "0|###",
        "1|",
        "2|#"
      ],
      "explain": "`Range(0, 3)` yields 0, 1, 2. `new string('#', 0)` is the empty string, so the middle bar is blank."
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "Set difference. What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "var required = new[] { \"Math\", \"Physics\", \"History\" };",
        "var taken = new[] { \"Physics\", \"Art\" };",
        "",
        "Console.WriteLine(string.Join(\", \", required.Except(taken)));"
      ],
      "answer": "Math, History",
      "explain": "`Except` keeps the items of `required` that are not in `taken`, in their original order. `Art` is only in the second sequence, so it plays no part."
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "var names = new[] { \"Math\", \"Physics\", \"Art\" };",
        "Console.WriteLine(names.Aggregate((acc, next) => acc + \", \" + next));"
      ],
      "options": [
        "`Math, Physics, Art`",
        "`MathPhysicsArt`",
        "`, Math, Physics, Art`",
        "`Art, Physics, Math`"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "With no seed, `Aggregate` starts from the first item and folds each next one in: `\"Math\"`, then `\"Math, Physics\"`, then `\"Math, Physics, Art\"`. It is a general fold; for joining strings `string.Join` says the same thing more plainly and also copes with an empty list, where seedless `Aggregate` throws."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Score bands",
      "carry": true,
      "prompt": [
        "At the end of the report, print an empty line, `Score bands:`, then five lines: `90s`, `80s`, `70s`, `60s` and `<60`.",
        "",
        "Each line is the label padded to 3 characters, a space, the count right-aligned in 2 characters, a space, and a bar of `#`, one per score: `$\"{label,-3} {count,2} {bar}\"`. A score of 100 counts in the 90s. Build the four decade bands with `Enumerable.Range`.",
        "",
